WEBVTT

00:00:25.358 --> 00:00:26.620
Good day to you.

00:00:26.693 --> 00:00:28.661
Good day to you.

00:00:32.032 --> 00:00:33.795
Good morning, Tom.

00:00:33.867 --> 00:00:35.459
Good morning, Grace.

00:00:35.535 --> 00:00:37.127
So, where are you heading?

00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:39.137
Uh, Sullivan's.
Care to join me?

00:00:39.205 --> 00:00:41.469
Oh, I wish I could,
but there's a bit of a problem.

00:00:41.541 --> 00:00:42.974
Oh, really?

00:00:43.043 --> 00:00:44.635
Well, you see,
Tommy, me boy,

00:00:44.711 --> 00:00:47.145
the good Lord blessed me
with a fine wife.

00:00:47.213 --> 00:00:48.976
I'll never forget
the day I met her.

00:00:49.049 --> 00:00:51.142
I was on me way
to the fair in Dooleen...

00:00:51.217 --> 00:00:52.844
or was it Kilkee?

00:00:52.919 --> 00:00:53.977
There's some fine

00:00:54.087 --> 00:00:55.987
trout fishing to be had
in Kilkee this time of year.

00:00:56.056 --> 00:00:57.990
Who said anything
about trout?

00:00:58.058 --> 00:00:59.958
Timothy Ryan,
God rest his soul.

00:01:00.026 --> 00:01:01.891
He was one for the trout.

00:01:01.961 --> 00:01:03.428
The poor man's
been dead a fortnight.

00:01:03.496 --> 00:01:04.793
Some say he had the croup,

00:01:04.864 --> 00:01:05.922
but don't you believe it.

00:01:05.999 --> 00:01:08.627
The widow Moore
gave him the evil eye.

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:10.362
And... your point?

00:01:10.437 --> 00:01:12.428
Me wife and l...
well, we've hit

00:01:12.505 --> 00:01:13.767
a bit of a rough patch.

00:01:13.840 --> 00:01:15.774
A better woman never walked
the face of the earth.

00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:17.469
She threw you out again.

00:01:17.544 --> 00:01:19.478
With nothing but the clothes
on me back.

00:01:19.546 --> 00:01:20.308
How much?

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:23.440
A shilling or two
should suffice.

00:01:23.516 --> 00:01:24.778
Keep the change.

00:01:24.851 --> 00:01:26.648
God bless you, Tommy, me boy.

00:01:35.028 --> 00:01:37.121
Harry...

00:01:37.197 --> 00:01:39.290
weren't you supposed
to meet me at Sullivan's?

00:01:39.365 --> 00:01:41.128
Oh, sorry,
I got distracted

00:01:41.201 --> 00:01:42.964
by the, uh, scenery.

00:01:43.036 --> 00:01:44.298
This is Maggie.

00:01:44.370 --> 00:01:46.634
We've met. Could you excuse us?

00:01:46.706 --> 00:01:48.298
Charmed to have met you, Harry.

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:50.308
Charmed.

00:01:50.376 --> 00:01:51.468
What's the hurry?

00:01:51.544 --> 00:01:52.806
A word to the wise...

00:01:52.879 --> 00:01:55.313
stay away from
Maggie O'Halloran.

00:01:55.381 --> 00:01:56.473
She's promised
to a pig farmer

00:01:56.549 --> 00:01:58.483
with a very large rake.

00:01:58.551 --> 00:01:59.381
Well, does she have a sister?

00:01:59.452 --> 00:02:00.350
Wooden teeth.

00:02:00.420 --> 00:02:01.409
Oh, nothing an little...

00:02:01.488 --> 00:02:02.682
adjustment
to the holo-matrix

00:02:02.755 --> 00:02:03.722
wouldn't fix.

00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:05.189
No, no, I'm not
changing a thing.

00:02:05.258 --> 00:02:07.590
Fair Haven is perfect
just the way it is.

00:02:07.660 --> 00:02:10.094
Tommy boy, you forgot
the leprechauns.

00:02:10.163 --> 00:02:11.425
Yeah. No.

00:02:11.498 --> 00:02:14.262
No leprechauns, no
aliens, no starships.

00:02:14.334 --> 00:02:17.098
I want this to be a place
where the crew can unwind.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.104
Morning, lads.

00:02:19.172 --> 00:02:21.766
Ah, heard any good
confessions lately?

00:02:21.841 --> 00:02:24.537
Doctor/patient confidentiality,
Mr. Paris.

00:02:24.611 --> 00:02:26.772
Harry hasn't seen
Sullivan's yet.

00:02:26.846 --> 00:02:27.938
Do you care to join us?

00:02:28.081 --> 00:02:29.275
Don't mind if I do.

00:02:29.349 --> 00:02:31.943
I'll need to leave
no later than 1300 hours.

00:02:32.018 --> 00:02:33.610
Medical emergency?

00:02:33.686 --> 00:02:34.618
Not exactly.

00:02:34.687 --> 00:02:37.884
I'm working on my homily
for Sunday's mass,

00:02:37.957 --> 00:02:39.254
and I expect
both you sinners...

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:41.850
to be in attendance.

00:02:43.863 --> 00:02:44.955
He's kidding, right?

00:02:45.031 --> 00:02:48.330
You wanted authenticity.

00:04:40.179 --> 00:04:42.272
Everybody, place your bets!

00:04:42.348 --> 00:04:43.781
Three bob on Liam.

00:04:43.850 --> 00:04:44.782
Three bob it is.

00:04:44.851 --> 00:04:46.113
Five shillings on Liam.

00:04:46.185 --> 00:04:47.948
You're going to hurt
Harry's feelings.

00:04:48.021 --> 00:04:49.613
Oh, very well.

00:04:49.689 --> 00:04:51.122
Two shillings on Mr. Kim,

00:04:51.190 --> 00:04:52.623
and I'll pray for a miracle.

00:04:52.692 --> 00:04:54.785
Excuse me, Father, but I needed
a bit of counsel.

00:04:54.861 --> 00:04:55.793
I'm off duty right now.

00:04:55.862 --> 00:04:57.955
But I've broken the
Fifth Commandment again.

00:04:58.031 --> 00:05:00.295
Say ten "Our Fathers"
and call me in the morning.

00:05:00.366 --> 00:05:02.095
- Oh, thank you, Father.
- Gentlemen.

00:05:04.637 --> 00:05:06.730
Come on, come on, Mr. Kim.

00:05:06.806 --> 00:05:09.001
Show him what for.
You can do it.

00:05:19.986 --> 00:05:21.419
What'll it be?

00:05:21.487 --> 00:05:23.079
I'm looking
for some friends of mine.

00:05:23.156 --> 00:05:24.589
Well, we're all friends here.

00:05:24.657 --> 00:05:26.921
Well, then,
have you seen Tom Paris?

00:05:27.026 --> 00:05:29.756
He's right over there
with young Harry.

00:05:29.829 --> 00:05:30.921
Poor sod.

00:05:30.997 --> 00:05:32.760
I'm afraid no
one's whipped Liam

00:05:32.832 --> 00:05:33.924
in three years.

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.764
There's a first time
for everything.

00:05:36.836 --> 00:05:38.770
An optimist, are you?

00:05:38.838 --> 00:05:41.204
A realist.

00:05:43.343 --> 00:05:45.538
Come on, Ensign, try!

00:05:45.611 --> 00:05:47.545
Try!

00:05:47.613 --> 00:05:50.548
Come on, me boy, push, push!

00:05:50.616 --> 00:05:51.947
I'm trying!

00:05:52.018 --> 00:05:54.486
Come on! Try harder!

00:05:54.554 --> 00:05:56.283
You got him! You got him, lad!
You got him!

00:06:00.827 --> 00:06:02.226
What?! What
in God's name?!

00:06:02.295 --> 00:06:03.592
Come on, Liam, push!

00:06:05.465 --> 00:06:06.955
Yes! Yes!

00:06:08.534 --> 00:06:10.559
I knew you could do it, Ensign.

00:06:10.636 --> 00:06:12.570
So, this is the program

00:06:12.638 --> 00:06:14.401
I've been hearing
so much about.

00:06:14.474 --> 00:06:16.066
Welcome, weary traveler.

00:06:16.142 --> 00:06:17.905
You have outdone yourself

00:06:17.977 --> 00:06:18.909
this time.

00:06:18.978 --> 00:06:20.240
Everything is authentic

00:06:20.313 --> 00:06:22.577
except for one tiny detail.

00:06:22.648 --> 00:06:23.910
Oh?

00:06:23.983 --> 00:06:26.850
The harp on the sign...
it's backwards.

00:06:28.688 --> 00:06:30.246
Oh. Everybody's a critic.

00:06:30.323 --> 00:06:31.654
As I recall, the Captain is

00:06:31.724 --> 00:06:33.919
quite an aficionado
of Irish history.

00:06:34.060 --> 00:06:35.254
I hate to break up the party,

00:06:35.328 --> 00:06:36.761
but we have some
business to attend to.

00:06:36.829 --> 00:06:38.763
There's a neutronic
wavefront approaching,

00:06:38.831 --> 00:06:40.264
class-9.

00:06:40.333 --> 00:06:41.357
Class-9?

00:06:42.435 --> 00:06:45.302
Sorry, boys, duty calls.

00:06:46.672 --> 00:06:49.266
Uh, "wavefront"?

00:06:49.342 --> 00:06:51.435
Now, what in the name
of God is that?

00:06:51.511 --> 00:06:54.275
Um, a wee bit
of bad weather.

00:06:54.347 --> 00:06:56.474
Oh.

00:07:08.594 --> 00:07:10.391
Borg classification 3472,

00:07:10.463 --> 00:07:12.397
partical density anomaly.

00:07:12.465 --> 00:07:13.557
Where'd it come from?

00:07:13.633 --> 00:07:15.396
I believe it was formed
by the collision

00:07:15.468 --> 00:07:17.060
of two neutron stars.

00:07:17.136 --> 00:07:18.569
The wavefront is traveling

00:07:18.638 --> 00:07:21.402
at a velocity of 200,000
kilometers per second,

00:07:21.474 --> 00:07:24.238
and it extends
for 3.6 light-years.

00:07:24.310 --> 00:07:27.074
How long before it hits?

00:07:27.146 --> 00:07:29.080
Approximately 15 hours.

00:07:29.148 --> 00:07:30.581
We're already
feeling its effects.

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:33.585
The neutron radiation
is disrupting plasma flow.

00:07:33.653 --> 00:07:34.915
We can't jump to warp.

00:07:34.987 --> 00:07:37.421
Impulse power won't be enough
to outrun that thing.

00:07:37.490 --> 00:07:39.424
Then we'll have
to ride it out.

00:07:39.492 --> 00:07:41.756
We'll generate an
inverse warp field

00:07:41.828 --> 00:07:43.090
and drop anchor.

00:07:43.162 --> 00:07:45.926
That should protect us
from the turbulence.

00:07:45.998 --> 00:07:47.590
What about the radiation?

00:07:47.667 --> 00:07:48.759
Lt'll only get worse.

00:07:48.835 --> 00:07:49.767
Have the Doctor

00:07:49.836 --> 00:07:51.269
prepare inoculations
for the crew.

00:07:51.337 --> 00:07:52.599
Go to Yellow Alert.

00:07:52.672 --> 00:07:53.934
Tom, B'Elanna, get started

00:07:54.006 --> 00:07:54.938
on converting the warp core.

00:07:55.007 --> 00:07:55.939
Yes, ma'am.

00:07:56.008 --> 00:07:58.476
Let's batten down the hatches.

00:08:06.619 --> 00:08:07.551
Captain.

00:08:07.620 --> 00:08:09.554
Just burning the midnight oil.

00:08:09.622 --> 00:08:11.214
Midnight's come and gone.

00:08:11.290 --> 00:08:13.224
Then it's time for a break.

00:08:13.292 --> 00:08:14.384
Do you mind?

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:16.587
I could use the company.

00:08:18.965 --> 00:08:20.557
This approaching wavefront...

00:08:20.633 --> 00:08:22.897
it's bringing back
some unpleasant memories.

00:08:22.969 --> 00:08:24.561
How so?

00:08:24.637 --> 00:08:26.571
You know I grew up
on a farm in Indiana.

00:08:26.639 --> 00:08:28.732
We used to have some
terrible thunderstorms

00:08:28.808 --> 00:08:30.241
during the summer months.

00:08:30.309 --> 00:08:31.901
At the first bolt of lightning,

00:08:31.978 --> 00:08:33.445
I'd bolt under the bed.

00:08:33.513 --> 00:08:36.414
We had some pretty nasty weather
on Talax, too.

00:08:36.482 --> 00:08:39.542
I-I always enjoyed
a good ion storm.

00:08:39.619 --> 00:08:41.382
Give me clear skies any day.

00:08:41.454 --> 00:08:43.581
Well, n-n-now
that you bring it up, uh,

00:08:43.656 --> 00:08:46.147
I am concerned
with keeping up morale

00:08:46.225 --> 00:08:47.590
over the next few days.

00:08:47.660 --> 00:08:49.924
The crew is not used
to sitting still.

00:08:49.996 --> 00:08:50.928
Suggestions?

00:08:50.997 --> 00:08:53.431
Everyone seems to love
Fair Haven.

00:08:53.499 --> 00:08:56.764
I was thinking we might initiate
an open-door protocol

00:08:56.836 --> 00:08:58.599
on the holodeck
and keep the program running

00:08:58.671 --> 00:09:01.868
24 hours a day, let people come
and go as they please.

00:09:01.941 --> 00:09:03.203
Permission granted.

00:09:03.276 --> 00:09:06.404
Fair Haven's just become
our port in the storm.

00:09:07.613 --> 00:09:10.377
I'm, uh, heading
down there myself

00:09:10.449 --> 00:09:11.711
if you'd like to join me.

00:09:11.784 --> 00:09:13.217
There's a charming little inn

00:09:13.286 --> 00:09:14.378
called The Ox and Lamb.

00:09:14.453 --> 00:09:16.216
The owner offered to share

00:09:16.289 --> 00:09:17.551
some of his recipes.

00:09:17.623 --> 00:09:19.887
No, thanks.
I still have work to do.

00:09:19.959 --> 00:09:22.587
Okay.

00:09:39.412 --> 00:09:41.209
What'll it be?

00:09:42.248 --> 00:09:44.614
A cup of tea would be nice.

00:09:44.684 --> 00:09:47.414
I just made one.

00:09:47.486 --> 00:09:50.455
- Cream?
- Please.

00:09:51.490 --> 00:09:55.927
So, what brings you
to Fair Haven, Miss?

00:09:55.995 --> 00:09:56.927
Kathryn.

00:09:56.996 --> 00:09:59.089
I'm just passing
through...

00:09:59.165 --> 00:10:01.258
on my way home.

00:10:01.334 --> 00:10:03.268
How long have you been
on the road?

00:10:03.336 --> 00:10:06.772
Five years, almost six.

00:10:06.839 --> 00:10:08.830
- You must be homesick.
- No.

00:10:08.908 --> 00:10:13.140
Sometimes. Thank you.

00:10:16.349 --> 00:10:18.476
"Cead mil failte."

00:10:18.551 --> 00:10:20.178
Translation?

00:10:20.252 --> 00:10:21.446
"A hundred thousand
welcomes."

00:10:21.520 --> 00:10:23.784
It's an old Irish saying.

00:10:23.856 --> 00:10:25.483
We're all friends here.

00:10:27.526 --> 00:10:30.791
I had an aunt who used to
have a saying like that.

00:10:30.863 --> 00:10:34.094
"A stranger is a friend
you just haven't met yet."

00:10:34.166 --> 00:10:35.292
Definitely Irish.

00:10:35.368 --> 00:10:37.427
She had an Irish temper, too.

00:10:37.503 --> 00:10:40.131
She and my uncle had
a place not far from here

00:10:40.206 --> 00:10:41.867
in County Clare.

00:10:41.941 --> 00:10:44.307
Ah... then you're
closer to home

00:10:44.377 --> 00:10:47.505
than you think, Katie O'Clare.

00:10:50.716 --> 00:10:52.343
You know, it's later
than I thought,

00:10:52.418 --> 00:10:53.817
and I've kept
you long enough.

00:10:53.886 --> 00:10:54.818
Oh, now, stay awhile.

00:10:54.887 --> 00:10:56.149
Didn't your auntie teach you

00:10:56.222 --> 00:10:59.214
that it's impolite to leave
without playing a game of rings?

00:10:59.291 --> 00:11:01.054
I really can't,
but thanks for the tea.

00:11:01.127 --> 00:11:02.685
Afraid you'd lose?

00:11:06.165 --> 00:11:08.360
I rarely lose.

00:11:09.435 --> 00:11:10.629
Prove it.

00:11:16.676 --> 00:11:18.906
One game.

00:11:18.978 --> 00:11:21.708
I'll set them up.

00:11:29.822 --> 00:11:30.754
Another ringer.

00:11:30.823 --> 00:11:32.120
Well, I'm not surprised.

00:11:32.191 --> 00:11:34.125
You stepped
over the beer stain.

00:11:34.193 --> 00:11:35.592
- I did not.
- You did, too,

00:11:35.661 --> 00:11:37.788
by half a boot,
and then you moved back,

00:11:37.863 --> 00:11:39.524
hoping I wouldn't notice.

00:11:39.598 --> 00:11:42.795
These boots are half a size
too large, so in reality,

00:11:42.868 --> 00:11:44.768
my toes never crossed the line.

00:11:44.837 --> 00:11:45.804
Your turn.

00:11:46.872 --> 00:11:48.703
For luck.

00:11:48.774 --> 00:11:50.503
Getting sweet with the rings
isn't going help you.

00:11:50.576 --> 00:11:52.806
We'll see.

00:11:54.246 --> 00:11:54.974
Damn!

00:11:55.081 --> 00:11:56.673
Devil won't help you, either.

00:11:56.749 --> 00:11:59.980
Well, maybe rings
aren't my forte after all.

00:12:00.086 --> 00:12:02.316
Would you care
to arm wrestle?

00:12:02.388 --> 00:12:04.253
That's not a woman's
game, Katie.

00:12:04.323 --> 00:12:05.255
You could get hurt.

00:12:05.324 --> 00:12:08.350
I'm stronger than I look.

00:12:08.427 --> 00:12:11.089
Ah!

00:12:11.163 --> 00:12:13.324
Now, that's quite
a grip you have.

00:12:13.399 --> 00:12:14.866
Not bad yourself.

00:12:17.837 --> 00:12:19.930
I couldn't help but notice
that you have your leg

00:12:20.005 --> 00:12:21.734
braced against the bar.

00:12:21.807 --> 00:12:25.504
Well, how else do you
expect me to win?

00:12:26.779 --> 00:12:29.179
Well, will we call it a draw?

00:12:30.249 --> 00:12:32.274
Sounds good to me.

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:37.084
We run a foot race
down to the station and back?

00:12:40.192 --> 00:12:42.160
It's good to make
a new friend.

00:12:42.228 --> 00:12:44.287
You have a nice way
about you.

00:12:44.363 --> 00:12:46.797
"Flattery's the
food of fools."

00:12:46.866 --> 00:12:48.424
Another pearl of wisdom
from your auntie?

00:12:48.501 --> 00:12:51.436
No. Jonathan Swift.

00:12:51.504 --> 00:12:53.369
Swift? Never heard of him.

00:12:53.439 --> 00:12:55.600
He was an author.

00:12:55.674 --> 00:12:56.971
I was never one for reading.

00:12:57.042 --> 00:12:59.510
Well, that's too bad.

00:12:59.578 --> 00:13:01.910
Some of the greatest writers
in the world are Irish.

00:13:01.981 --> 00:13:05.576
Well, they say that Dr. Gilroy
has a library of books,

00:13:05.651 --> 00:13:07.915
and, well, next time
I see him,

00:13:07.987 --> 00:13:11.081
I'll ask him can I borrow
one or two?

00:13:11.157 --> 00:13:12.749
Good mornin'.

00:13:12.825 --> 00:13:15.919
Good mornin'.

00:13:15.995 --> 00:13:19.431
Oh, my God, will you
look at the time?

00:13:19.498 --> 00:13:21.591
Frannie, come here.

00:13:21.667 --> 00:13:24.101
There's someone
I want you to meet.

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:27.435
Katie O'Clare, this is my wife,

00:13:27.506 --> 00:13:28.438
Francis.

00:13:28.507 --> 00:13:29.872
Pleased to meet you.

00:13:29.942 --> 00:13:32.069
I hope Michael hasn't been
bending your ear

00:13:32.144 --> 00:13:34.544
- all night long.
- My ear, my elbow...

00:13:34.613 --> 00:13:36.581
We were arm wrestling.

00:13:36.649 --> 00:13:38.583
Mmm, such a gentleman.

00:13:38.651 --> 00:13:40.551
Well, thank you very much

00:13:40.619 --> 00:13:41.813
for your hospitality,

00:13:41.887 --> 00:13:43.821
and now I really
must be leaving.

00:13:43.889 --> 00:13:47.325
And, uh, drop in again
before you leave town.

00:13:47.393 --> 00:13:49.327
I will!

00:13:53.732 --> 00:13:55.723
If you experience
any dizziness,

00:13:55.801 --> 00:13:57.735
report to Sick Bay immediately.

00:13:57.803 --> 00:13:58.770
Thank you.

00:13:59.839 --> 00:14:01.306
Good morning, gentlemen.

00:14:01.373 --> 00:14:03.898
I believe it's
afternoon... oversleep?

00:14:04.009 --> 00:14:05.909
- Holodeck.
- Fair Haven?

00:14:06.011 --> 00:14:07.273
Welcome, weary traveler.

00:14:07.346 --> 00:14:08.438
Even I have to admit...

00:14:08.514 --> 00:14:11.278
Mr. Paris' latest effort
is quite a tour de force.

00:14:11.350 --> 00:14:13.113
Ooh, high praise
from a hologram.

00:14:13.185 --> 00:14:15.278
Oh, I was thinking, Captain...

00:14:15.354 --> 00:14:17.788
now that we've got
this open-door policy,

00:14:17.857 --> 00:14:21.793
maybe I could expand Fair Haven
into Holodeck 2.

00:14:21.861 --> 00:14:23.624
It would give me some room
to create the seacoast.

00:14:23.696 --> 00:14:25.926
- By all means.
- Speaking of revisions,

00:14:26.031 --> 00:14:28.932
I was hoping I could give
my character a more active role.

00:14:29.034 --> 00:14:31.935
In the period you've created,
the village priest was

00:14:32.037 --> 00:14:33.470
the most prominent member
of the community,

00:14:33.539 --> 00:14:35.131
held in the highest regard.

00:14:35.207 --> 00:14:37.641
You know, that's
a great idea, Doc.

00:14:37.710 --> 00:14:40.645
We could send Father Mulligan

00:14:40.713 --> 00:14:42.806
on a retreat
to a nearby monastery,

00:14:42.882 --> 00:14:45.316
where he takes
a vow of silence

00:14:45.384 --> 00:14:47.352
and never speaks again.

00:14:49.054 --> 00:14:50.487
Try it!

00:14:50.556 --> 00:14:52.319
And you'll be saying
"Hail Marys"

00:14:52.391 --> 00:14:54.689
till St. Patrick's Day.

00:14:58.230 --> 00:14:59.162
Time?

00:14:59.231 --> 00:15:00.664
30 seconds.

00:15:00.733 --> 00:15:03.463
Let's see it.

00:15:09.508 --> 00:15:10.907
All hands, this is

00:15:11.043 --> 00:15:11.907
the Captain.

00:15:12.011 --> 00:15:15.970
Secure your stations
and brace for impact.

00:15:25.925 --> 00:15:27.620
We've cleared the leading edge.

00:15:27.693 --> 00:15:29.388
Turbulence is decreasing.

00:15:29.461 --> 00:15:30.792
Shields are holding.

00:15:30.863 --> 00:15:32.558
- Damage?
- We've got

00:15:32.631 --> 00:15:34.622
a ruptured plasma conduit,
Deck 9.

00:15:34.700 --> 00:15:36.634
Send a repair team.

00:15:36.702 --> 00:15:39.933
Maintain Yellow Alert.

00:15:40.039 --> 00:15:43.600
Let's hope that was
the worst of it.

00:15:43.676 --> 00:15:45.803
Captain's Personal Log.

00:15:45.878 --> 00:15:48.312
It's been ten hours
since the storm hit.

00:15:48.380 --> 00:15:51.645
We estimate another three days
before we're clear of it.

00:15:51.717 --> 00:15:54.481
The crew's in good spirits,
and many of them

00:15:54.553 --> 00:15:57.488
have taken the opportunity
to visit Fair Haven.

00:15:57.556 --> 00:16:00.992
I met an interesting man there,
and for a while,

00:16:01.060 --> 00:16:03.426
I almost forgot
he was a hologram.

00:16:03.495 --> 00:16:05.759
We weren't exactly compatible.

00:16:05.831 --> 00:16:08.595
Then again, Mr. Paris
didn't program him

00:16:08.667 --> 00:16:11.295
to my specifications.

00:16:22.181 --> 00:16:29.314
Computer, display Fair Haven
character Michael Sullivan.

00:16:31.924 --> 00:16:37.920
Adjust his parameters to the
following specifications...

00:16:38.030 --> 00:16:42.296
Give him the education
of a 19th century...

00:16:42.368 --> 00:16:46.327
third-year student
at Trinity College.

00:16:47.873 --> 00:16:50.467
Modification complete.

00:16:50.542 --> 00:16:55.673
Now... access the character's
interactive subroutines.

00:16:57.049 --> 00:16:59.313
Make him more provocative.

00:16:59.385 --> 00:17:01.376
Specify.

00:17:01.453 --> 00:17:04.889
Give him a more
complicated personality.

00:17:04.990 --> 00:17:06.617
Specify.

00:17:09.828 --> 00:17:11.693
More outspoken...

00:17:11.764 --> 00:17:14.892
more confident,
not so reserved...

00:17:15.034 --> 00:17:18.561
and make him more curious
about the world around him.

00:17:18.637 --> 00:17:21.162
Modification complete.

00:17:21.240 --> 00:17:26.405
Good. Now...

00:17:26.478 --> 00:17:30.574
increase the character's
height by...

00:17:30.649 --> 00:17:32.378
three centimeters.

00:17:35.988 --> 00:17:37.853
Remove the facial hair.

00:17:39.725 --> 00:17:40.657
No...

00:17:40.726 --> 00:17:41.750
no, I don't like that.

00:17:41.827 --> 00:17:44.796
Put some back...
about two days' growth.

00:17:45.864 --> 00:17:46.922
Better.

00:17:47.032 --> 00:17:49.432
Oh, one more thing.

00:17:49.501 --> 00:17:52.937
Access his interpersonal
subroutines...

00:17:53.005 --> 00:17:55.940
familial characters.

00:17:56.008 --> 00:17:59.273
Delete the wife.

00:17:59.344 --> 00:18:02.245
Modification complete.

00:18:06.118 --> 00:18:09.645
Pleased to meet you,
Mr. Sullivan.

00:18:24.636 --> 00:18:26.228
Are you ill, Commander?

00:18:27.639 --> 00:18:31.905
I am experiencing
a slight loss of equilibrium

00:18:31.977 --> 00:18:35.276
and some gastrointestinal
distress.

00:18:35.347 --> 00:18:36.837
Space sickness?

00:18:36.915 --> 00:18:40.078
Unlikely. I'm not prone
to that condition.

00:18:40.152 --> 00:18:41.915
Perhaps you should
go to Sick Bay.

00:18:41.987 --> 00:18:43.420
I'll be fine.

00:18:43.489 --> 00:18:45.753
I'm telling you,
we should add more fog.

00:18:45.824 --> 00:18:47.416
Fog is depressing.

00:18:47.493 --> 00:18:48.425
It's authentic!

00:18:48.494 --> 00:18:49.756
It's dangerous!

00:18:49.828 --> 00:18:51.420
All right, we could add
a lighthouse.

00:18:51.497 --> 00:18:54.398
It's Fair Haven, Harry.
Sunshine.

00:18:54.466 --> 00:18:55.398
Tuvok,

00:18:55.467 --> 00:18:57.298
what do you think:
Irish seacoast...

00:18:57.369 --> 00:18:58.768
fog or no fog?

00:18:58.837 --> 00:19:00.270
I have no opinion.

00:19:00.339 --> 00:19:02.773
He hasn't visited our
little paradise yet.

00:19:02.841 --> 00:19:03.773
Nor do I

00:19:03.842 --> 00:19:04.774
intend to.

00:19:04.843 --> 00:19:06.470
I think you'd like
it, Commander.

00:19:06.545 --> 00:19:07.477
It's a great place
to meditate.

00:19:07.546 --> 00:19:09.537
Imagine yourself sitting

00:19:09.615 --> 00:19:11.879
high on a bluff
overlooking the ocean.

00:19:11.950 --> 00:19:13.542
Salt air...

00:19:13.619 --> 00:19:16.452
the rhythm of the waves
rising and crashing

00:19:16.522 --> 00:19:17.716
against the rocks...

00:19:17.789 --> 00:19:19.051
a tiny fishing boat

00:19:19.124 --> 00:19:20.557
bobbing on the water below...

00:19:20.626 --> 00:19:21.718
up and down,

00:19:21.793 --> 00:19:23.260
up and down.

00:19:24.329 --> 00:19:27.457
I get the idea, Ensign.
Thank you.

00:19:27.533 --> 00:19:30.229
Mutton, creamed cabbage
or blood pudding?

00:19:30.302 --> 00:19:31.667
Explain.

00:19:31.737 --> 00:19:33.398
I'm preparing
a traditional Irish meal

00:19:33.472 --> 00:19:34.734
at The Ox and Lamb
this afternoon,

00:19:34.806 --> 00:19:37.400
and I cannot decide
on a main course.

00:19:37.476 --> 00:19:39.410
Blood pudding...
you can't lose.

00:19:39.478 --> 00:19:40.809
Well, that was

00:19:40.879 --> 00:19:41.811
my first choice, too,

00:19:41.880 --> 00:19:43.313
but replicating
the lamb's intestines...

00:19:43.382 --> 00:19:45.873
it-it could be tricky,
and every time

00:19:45.951 --> 00:19:48.419
I try to heat the blood,
it coagulates

00:19:48.487 --> 00:19:49.419
in the milk.

00:19:49.488 --> 00:19:50.580
If you'll excuse me.

00:19:50.656 --> 00:19:54.023
I think I will consult
the Doctor.

00:20:17.849 --> 00:20:18.781
Excuse me, ma'am.

00:20:18.850 --> 00:20:19.782
Hmm?

00:20:19.851 --> 00:20:21.284
Have you seen
Michael Sullivan?

00:20:21.353 --> 00:20:22.445
Oh, you might try the pub.

00:20:22.521 --> 00:20:23.613
I just came from there.

00:20:23.689 --> 00:20:26.453
Oh, then he'll be
at the train station.

00:20:26.525 --> 00:20:27.457
Yeah.

00:20:27.526 --> 00:20:28.515
Thank you.

00:20:28.594 --> 00:20:29.561
Sure.

00:20:41.540 --> 00:20:42.973
Excuse me, sir.

00:20:43.041 --> 00:20:45.509
Is the train to Galway
running on time?

00:20:45.577 --> 00:20:48.808
Um... I'm afraid
you've just missed it.

00:20:48.880 --> 00:20:51.348
Have a seat.
Wait for the next one.

00:20:56.555 --> 00:20:59.422
Do you know Jane Eldon?

00:20:59.491 --> 00:21:02.688
Eldon. No, I've never met her.

00:21:02.761 --> 00:21:04.422
Well, I'd be terrified
if you had.

00:21:04.496 --> 00:21:05.861
She's been dead 70 years.

00:21:08.834 --> 00:21:10.267
No, I was, uh...

00:21:10.335 --> 00:21:12.599
I was thinking
about her poetry.

00:21:12.671 --> 00:21:14.605
It's too pastoral
for my taste.

00:21:14.673 --> 00:21:16.106
Don't you agree?

00:21:16.174 --> 00:21:18.870
I'm not familiar
with her work.

00:21:18.944 --> 00:21:20.434
Really?

00:21:20.512 --> 00:21:23.037
Well, what about Sean Gogarty?

00:21:23.115 --> 00:21:25.811
They have similar
rhyming schemes.

00:21:29.187 --> 00:21:31.155
You've some
catching up to do.

00:21:32.691 --> 00:21:34.625
Well, I'm here
every afternoon.

00:21:34.693 --> 00:21:36.752
You should join me sometime.

00:21:36.828 --> 00:21:38.796
I tried talking
poetry with Seamus,

00:21:38.864 --> 00:21:41.458
but all he can do
is recite limericks.

00:21:41.533 --> 00:21:42.465
I'd love to.

00:21:42.534 --> 00:21:44.729
Strange place to read, though.

00:21:44.803 --> 00:21:46.134
Not at all.

00:21:46.204 --> 00:21:48.638
I love the sound of the
trains coming and going.

00:21:48.707 --> 00:21:50.299
Gets me thinking
about places

00:21:50.375 --> 00:21:52.639
I'd like to visit.

00:21:52.711 --> 00:21:55.305
Have you traveled
much yourself?

00:21:55.380 --> 00:21:57.644
As a matter of fact...

00:21:57.716 --> 00:21:59.809
But there's one place
I haven't been yet...

00:21:59.885 --> 00:22:00.977
Castle O'Dell.

00:22:01.053 --> 00:22:03.578
It's a steep climb,

00:22:03.655 --> 00:22:05.247
but from the top
of the battlement,

00:22:05.324 --> 00:22:07.258
you can see all
the way to Dublin.

00:22:07.326 --> 00:22:09.055
We'd better be moving.

00:22:16.468 --> 00:22:18.265
They say when the sun
goes down,

00:22:18.337 --> 00:22:20.771
the King of the Faeries
reclaims the castle.

00:22:20.839 --> 00:22:22.773
Maybe he'll invite
us to supper.

00:22:22.841 --> 00:22:25.435
Eh, you'll be dining alone,
without me.

00:22:25.510 --> 00:22:27.603
One taste
of the faeries' banquet,

00:22:27.679 --> 00:22:29.271
and you'll never return
to this world.

00:22:29.348 --> 00:22:31.612
Oh, don't tell me you
believe those stories.

00:22:31.683 --> 00:22:36.279
Believe? No.
But I do respect them.

00:22:36.355 --> 00:22:38.118
Can I ask you something,
Katie?

00:22:38.190 --> 00:22:39.623
Please.

00:22:39.691 --> 00:22:42.785
Have you a man
waiting for you at home?

00:22:42.861 --> 00:22:44.123
No.

00:22:44.196 --> 00:22:45.823
Are you looking for one?

00:22:46.865 --> 00:22:48.628
Why, do you have somebody?

00:22:48.700 --> 00:22:49.962
In Fair Haven?

00:22:50.035 --> 00:22:52.469
Not unless you fancy
a pig farmer.

00:22:52.537 --> 00:22:55.131
Oh, not my type.

00:22:55.207 --> 00:22:57.198
Well, what about a barkeep

00:22:57.275 --> 00:23:01.371
who reads poetry
in strange places?

00:23:01.446 --> 00:23:02.606
Captain?

00:23:04.282 --> 00:23:05.840
I thought that was you.

00:23:05.917 --> 00:23:07.908
Just getting in the spirit.

00:23:08.019 --> 00:23:09.748
I can see that.

00:23:09.821 --> 00:23:11.550
I don't believe we've met.

00:23:11.623 --> 00:23:14.148
Sullivan.
Michael Sullivan.

00:23:14.226 --> 00:23:15.158
Chakotay.

00:23:15.227 --> 00:23:16.626
That's a fine tattoo.

00:23:16.695 --> 00:23:17.923
Are you off a ship?

00:23:18.063 --> 00:23:19.928
You could say that.

00:23:20.999 --> 00:23:22.762
We're on the way up
to the old castle.

00:23:22.834 --> 00:23:24.927
You're welcome
to come with us.

00:23:25.070 --> 00:23:26.935
Thanks, but, um...

00:23:27.005 --> 00:23:29.599
I'm meeting Neelix
at The Ox and Lamb.

00:23:29.674 --> 00:23:31.869
You two have fun.

00:23:34.179 --> 00:23:38.775
Is it my imagination,
or did he call you Captain?

00:23:38.850 --> 00:23:40.977
Did he?

00:23:45.891 --> 00:23:47.825
Looks like the worst
is yet to come.

00:23:47.893 --> 00:23:49.326
Commander?

00:23:49.394 --> 00:23:50.759
I just came
from Astrometrics.

00:23:50.829 --> 00:23:53.263
Seven's found an increase
in the neutronic gradient

00:23:53.331 --> 00:23:55.094
at the trailing edge
of the wavefront.

00:23:55.167 --> 00:23:57.192
It's going to be
a rough ride when it hits.

00:23:57.269 --> 00:23:58.634
We've still got two days.

00:23:58.703 --> 00:24:01.069
Start working on a
new shield modulation.

00:24:01.139 --> 00:24:02.731
Yes, ma'am.

00:24:02.808 --> 00:24:04.799
That could explain
my recent space sickness.

00:24:04.876 --> 00:24:06.741
Vulcan physiology
is highly sensitive

00:24:06.812 --> 00:24:07.870
to neutronic gradients.

00:24:07.946 --> 00:24:09.914
You'd make a good barometer,
Tuvok.

00:24:09.981 --> 00:24:12.677
Every time you get queasy,
we go to Red Alert.

00:24:15.987 --> 00:24:17.614
Thanks for your report,
Commander.

00:24:17.689 --> 00:24:19.418
Don't mention it.

00:24:19.491 --> 00:24:22.426
Hills Most Green,
Hearts Unseen.

00:24:22.494 --> 00:24:23.586
Yes. Jane Eldon.

00:24:23.662 --> 00:24:25.061
Catching up
on a little reading.

00:24:25.130 --> 00:24:26.062
Those hills and hearts

00:24:26.131 --> 00:24:27.894
wouldn't happen to be
in Ireland?

00:24:27.966 --> 00:24:30.434
You can wipe that smirk
off your face.

00:24:30.502 --> 00:24:31.696
It's not what you think.

00:24:31.770 --> 00:24:35.433
I wasn't thinking anything,
but now that you mention it...

00:24:35.507 --> 00:24:39.273
I have an interest
in Irish culture.

00:24:39.344 --> 00:24:40.675
It's understandable.

00:24:40.745 --> 00:24:43.236
They've produced great writers
for hundreds of years...

00:24:43.315 --> 00:24:45.408
not to mention great bartenders.

00:24:47.619 --> 00:24:49.416
He's a hologram.

00:24:49.488 --> 00:24:52.753
I couldn't help but notice...
he seemed a little taller

00:24:52.824 --> 00:24:53.950
than the last
time I saw him.

00:24:54.025 --> 00:24:55.390
Yes, I made
a few modifications.

00:24:55.460 --> 00:24:56.392
In the interest

00:24:56.461 --> 00:24:57.450
of Irish culture?

00:24:57.529 --> 00:24:58.621
Exactly.

00:25:01.132 --> 00:25:03.464
You seemed embarrassed
when I ran into you.

00:25:03.535 --> 00:25:05.230
There was no reason to be.

00:25:05.303 --> 00:25:08.761
It was nice to see you
having a little fun.

00:25:12.978 --> 00:25:15.242
He is rather charming,
isn't he?

00:25:15.313 --> 00:25:18.476
Too bad he's made
of photons and force fields.

00:25:18.550 --> 00:25:21.610
I never let that
stand in my way.

00:25:30.495 --> 00:25:32.087
The boys are getting tired.

00:25:32.163 --> 00:25:33.892
Them or you?

00:25:33.965 --> 00:25:36.297
Faster, boys!

00:26:14.806 --> 00:26:16.535
Computer...

00:26:16.608 --> 00:26:19.805
remove all characters
except for Michael Sullivan.

00:26:27.218 --> 00:26:29.652
Can I ask you something, Katie?

00:26:29.721 --> 00:26:31.382
Please.

00:26:31.456 --> 00:26:33.720
Would you mind if I kissed you?

00:26:34.859 --> 00:26:37.123
I might even kiss you back.

00:26:39.497 --> 00:26:42.193
There's no point in
waiting any longer,

00:26:42.267 --> 00:26:43.564
is there?

00:26:43.635 --> 00:26:46.103
Oh, I think
I've waited long enough.

00:27:00.118 --> 00:27:02.211
Is there something wrong?

00:27:06.625 --> 00:27:09.253
No.

00:27:49.000 --> 00:27:51.298
Computer, recycle.

00:27:56.007 --> 00:27:56.974
Come in.

00:28:00.011 --> 00:28:01.706
I thought you'd like to know

00:28:01.780 --> 00:28:04.374
we've organized a rings
tournament tonight

00:28:04.449 --> 00:28:05.381
at Sullivan's.

00:28:05.450 --> 00:28:07.884
Thanks, Neelix,
but I have work to do.

00:28:10.755 --> 00:28:14.213
The Doctor's going to sing
something called "Danny Boy",

00:28:14.292 --> 00:28:17.784
and Mossie Donegan's promised
to bring his talking pig.

00:28:17.862 --> 00:28:18.988
Well, let's just say

00:28:19.064 --> 00:28:21.259
I'd rather stick to
reality right now.

00:28:22.967 --> 00:28:25.595
1900 hours...
if you change your mind.

00:28:25.670 --> 00:28:27.262
Thank you.

00:28:33.378 --> 00:28:34.902
Saints preserve us.

00:28:35.046 --> 00:28:38.482
I possess superior
hand/eye coordination.

00:28:38.550 --> 00:28:41.110
That's not all
that's superior.

00:28:41.186 --> 00:28:43.916
The lily and the rose
are staging

00:28:43.988 --> 00:28:46.218
a competition on your face.

00:28:46.291 --> 00:28:47.622
Clarify.

00:28:47.692 --> 00:28:49.216
The fullness of your lips

00:28:49.294 --> 00:28:51.387
and the paleness
of your cheeks...

00:28:51.463 --> 00:28:53.488
it's enough to make a man faint.

00:28:53.565 --> 00:28:57.092
Then, in that case,
perhaps we should sit down.

00:29:02.173 --> 00:29:03.470
I don't believe it.

00:29:03.541 --> 00:29:06.533
It's called
"old-world charm," Harry.

00:29:06.611 --> 00:29:07.976
What'll it be, gentlemen?

00:29:09.047 --> 00:29:10.207
Nothing for me.

00:29:10.281 --> 00:29:12.044
"Temperance is a virtue."

00:29:12.117 --> 00:29:13.414
Where's Michael?

00:29:13.485 --> 00:29:14.611
Over there.

00:29:18.690 --> 00:29:19.884
That's strange.

00:29:20.024 --> 00:29:22.356
I programmed him
not to drink.

00:29:22.427 --> 00:29:25.396
Must be a glitch
in his subroutine.

00:29:30.435 --> 00:29:32.699
Are these seats taken?

00:29:32.771 --> 00:29:34.932
Sit anywhere you like.

00:29:40.645 --> 00:29:44.877
Vile.

00:29:44.949 --> 00:29:48.112
It's been 15 years
since I touched the stuff.

00:29:48.186 --> 00:29:50.120
You making up for lost time?

00:29:50.188 --> 00:29:52.622
I was hoping
it might ease the pain.

00:29:52.690 --> 00:29:53.884
Are you in discomfort?

00:29:53.958 --> 00:29:56.620
Agony's more like it.

00:29:57.996 --> 00:30:01.955
How could you do this to me,
Lord?

00:30:03.334 --> 00:30:05.165
Why don't you ask him?

00:30:05.236 --> 00:30:06.897
You've got his ear, don't you?

00:30:06.971 --> 00:30:08.370
Well...

00:30:09.440 --> 00:30:11.101
Three days...

00:30:11.176 --> 00:30:14.270
the happiest three days
of my life.

00:30:14.345 --> 00:30:18.304
I was a fool to think
she felt the same.

00:30:19.951 --> 00:30:20.883
I was such a fool!

00:30:20.952 --> 00:30:22.419
Aw, sit down, Sullivan.

00:30:22.487 --> 00:30:24.421
Shut your mouth, man,
or I'll shut it for you.

00:30:24.489 --> 00:30:25.421
Oh, you will, will you?

00:30:25.490 --> 00:30:26.422
Hey, hey, hey...

00:30:26.491 --> 00:30:28.186
take it easy.

00:30:30.995 --> 00:30:35.091
Um... why don't you tell us
what happened?

00:30:36.701 --> 00:30:39.602
We...

00:30:39.671 --> 00:30:43.607
we spent a perfect day
together by the lake.

00:30:43.675 --> 00:30:45.643
I drifted off to sleep,

00:30:45.710 --> 00:30:49.510
and... when I woke up,
she was gone.

00:30:52.050 --> 00:30:53.415
Where is she, Tom?

00:30:53.484 --> 00:30:54.974
Who?

00:30:55.119 --> 00:30:56.586
Katie O'Clare... who else?

00:30:57.989 --> 00:30:59.581
Katie O'Clare?

00:30:59.657 --> 00:31:01.181
Your friend.

00:31:03.962 --> 00:31:05.725
Um...

00:31:05.797 --> 00:31:07.458
are you sure you didn't...

00:31:07.532 --> 00:31:10.695
uh... misinterpret
her interests?

00:31:10.768 --> 00:31:12.065
I mean, we're all friends here.

00:31:12.136 --> 00:31:14.195
Maybe she was just
being friendly.

00:31:14.272 --> 00:31:15.569
You calling me a liar?

00:31:15.640 --> 00:31:17.540
N-no, no, not at all.

00:31:17.609 --> 00:31:21.875
I thought...
Katie and me were in love.

00:31:21.946 --> 00:31:24.779
I may have to do
some reprogramming...

00:31:24.849 --> 00:31:25.907
What's the matter?

00:31:25.984 --> 00:31:27.315
You don't think
I'm good enough for her?

00:31:27.385 --> 00:31:28.909
No, I-I didn't say that.

00:31:28.987 --> 00:31:30.750
Well, tell me
where she's gone.

00:31:30.822 --> 00:31:31.720
I don't know.

00:31:31.789 --> 00:31:33.017
Well, I think you do!

00:31:36.294 --> 00:31:37.420
Gentlemen, please!

00:31:37.495 --> 00:31:39.463
"Love thy neighbor."

00:31:43.067 --> 00:31:44.364
Let's see... I remember

00:31:44.435 --> 00:31:46.699
trying to reach the
holodeck controls.

00:31:46.771 --> 00:31:48.796
Then, uh, somebody
grabbed my leg.

00:31:48.873 --> 00:31:50.864
Maybe it was the talking pig.

00:31:50.942 --> 00:31:55.208
If it was, he had
one hell of a left hook.

00:31:55.280 --> 00:31:56.872
What's all this?

00:31:56.948 --> 00:31:59.974
I'm afraid there's
been some trouble in paradise...

00:32:00.051 --> 00:32:02.076
an altercation in the pub
this afternoon.

00:32:02.153 --> 00:32:03.586
Several crewmen were injured.

00:32:03.655 --> 00:32:04.917
Nothing serious.

00:32:04.989 --> 00:32:07.423
Arm wrestling get
out of hand, boys?

00:32:07.492 --> 00:32:08.754
Not exactly.

00:32:08.826 --> 00:32:09.918
It was Michael Sullivan,
Captain.

00:32:09.994 --> 00:32:12.292
He was...

00:32:12.363 --> 00:32:14.627
...looking for someone.

00:32:14.699 --> 00:32:19.466
Why don't we...
take a little walk?

00:32:23.274 --> 00:32:26.766
Let me guess...
that someone is me.

00:32:26.844 --> 00:32:28.937
I don't mean to pry, Captain,

00:32:29.013 --> 00:32:30.776
but we've got a
broken-hearted hologram

00:32:30.848 --> 00:32:32.782
who believed that the
two of you were in love.

00:32:32.850 --> 00:32:35.444
Oh, I was sure he'd be
on to the next lass by now.

00:32:35.520 --> 00:32:36.953
I hope he's all right.

00:32:37.021 --> 00:32:38.613
Far from it.

00:32:38.690 --> 00:32:41.124
The fight spilled
out onto the street.

00:32:41.192 --> 00:32:42.625
Before long, he'd
climbed up a tree

00:32:42.694 --> 00:32:44.628
and began shouting your name.

00:32:44.696 --> 00:32:47.631
Mr. Neelix managed
to... talk him down.

00:32:47.699 --> 00:32:50.634
It could be a malfunction
in his behavioral subroutines.

00:32:50.702 --> 00:32:51.634
I've already checked that.

00:32:51.703 --> 00:32:52.965
His subroutines are fine...

00:32:53.037 --> 00:32:54.800
but I did notice you've made

00:32:54.872 --> 00:32:56.965
quite a number of alterations
to his program.

00:32:57.041 --> 00:32:57.973
Minor improvements.

00:32:58.042 --> 00:32:59.475
To make him more appealing?

00:32:59.544 --> 00:33:01.409
You're starting to pry, Doctor.

00:33:01.479 --> 00:33:04.073
I apologize for
overstepping my bounds,

00:33:04.148 --> 00:33:06.082
but I'm worried about you.

00:33:06.150 --> 00:33:07.913
Michael Sullivan
is a hologram.

00:33:07.986 --> 00:33:09.248
His broken heart can be mended

00:33:09.320 --> 00:33:10.252
with the flick of a switch.

00:33:10.321 --> 00:33:11.754
Your feelings, however,

00:33:11.823 --> 00:33:15.418
are a little more...
complicated.

00:33:15.493 --> 00:33:17.085
I'm not going to be
climbing any trees,

00:33:17.161 --> 00:33:19.595
if that's what you're
worried about.

00:33:19.664 --> 00:33:21.757
If you decide
you want to talk,

00:33:21.833 --> 00:33:25.428
I've been hearing a lot
of confessions lately.

00:33:25.503 --> 00:33:27.767
Let me know.

00:33:27.839 --> 00:33:31.468
You want a confession,
Doctor?

00:33:33.511 --> 00:33:36.378
All right.

00:33:38.683 --> 00:33:40.446
I've become
romantically involved

00:33:40.518 --> 00:33:42.611
with a hologram,
if that's possible.

00:33:42.687 --> 00:33:43.779
Tell me what happened.

00:33:43.855 --> 00:33:45.789
Oh, you know the story...
girl meets boy;

00:33:45.857 --> 00:33:47.449
girl modifies
boy's subroutines.

00:33:47.525 --> 00:33:49.117
Did you have
intimate relations?

00:33:49.193 --> 00:33:50.285
That's none
of your business.

00:33:50.361 --> 00:33:53.125
Let's just say it was
a memorable three days.

00:33:53.197 --> 00:33:54.459
I don't see the problem.

00:33:54.532 --> 00:33:55.624
Don't you?

00:33:55.700 --> 00:33:57.793
Michael Sullivan
is exactly my type...

00:33:57.869 --> 00:33:59.461
attractive, intelligent.

00:33:59.537 --> 00:34:00.970
We share
the same interests,

00:34:01.039 --> 00:34:02.734
and if there's something
I don't like,

00:34:02.807 --> 00:34:04.570
I can simply change it.

00:34:04.642 --> 00:34:06.234
I've noticed that
humans usually try

00:34:06.310 --> 00:34:08.073
to change the people
they fall in love with.

00:34:08.146 --> 00:34:09.078
What's the difference?

00:34:09.147 --> 00:34:12.207
In this case, it works.

00:34:15.186 --> 00:34:17.586
We had a picnic by the lake
yesterday afternoon.

00:34:17.655 --> 00:34:18.917
Michael drifted off
to sleep.

00:34:18.990 --> 00:34:20.753
His head was lying
on my shoulder,

00:34:20.825 --> 00:34:23.919
and I remember thinking,
"This is close to perfect."

00:34:23.995 --> 00:34:25.758
Then he began to snore.

00:34:25.830 --> 00:34:27.821
Did I nudge him with my elbow,

00:34:27.899 --> 00:34:29.423
hoping he'd roll over
and stop?

00:34:29.500 --> 00:34:31.764
Did I whisper in his ear
to wake him? No.

00:34:31.836 --> 00:34:34.600
Why bother, when I could
simply access the computer

00:34:34.672 --> 00:34:35.900
and alter
his vocal algorithms?

00:34:35.973 --> 00:34:38.407
And that's exactly
what I was about to do,

00:34:38.476 --> 00:34:40.103
when I realized
that everything around me

00:34:40.178 --> 00:34:43.443
was an illusion,
including him, so I left.

00:34:43.514 --> 00:34:45.448
Oh, I almost wrote him a note
to say good-bye.

00:34:45.516 --> 00:34:46.448
Could you believe that...

00:34:46.517 --> 00:34:49.281
a "Dear John" letter
to a hologram?

00:34:52.356 --> 00:34:55.450
I understand your
trepidation...

00:34:55.526 --> 00:34:57.460
but you're the Captain.

00:34:57.528 --> 00:34:58.790
You can't have a relationship

00:34:58.863 --> 00:34:59.955
with a member of your crew.

00:35:00.031 --> 00:35:01.726
They're all your subordinates.

00:35:01.799 --> 00:35:03.061
So where does that leave you...

00:35:03.134 --> 00:35:06.228
the occasional dalliance
with a passing alien?

00:35:06.304 --> 00:35:08.067
Voyager could be in
the Delta Quadrant

00:35:08.139 --> 00:35:09.902
for a very long time.

00:35:09.974 --> 00:35:12.067
A hologram may be
the only logical alternative.

00:35:12.143 --> 00:35:13.576
He's not real.

00:35:13.644 --> 00:35:15.077
He's as real as I am.

00:35:15.146 --> 00:35:17.273
Photons and force fields;
flesh and blood...

00:35:17.348 --> 00:35:18.280
it's all the same,

00:35:18.349 --> 00:35:20.283
as long as your
feelings are real.

00:35:20.351 --> 00:35:21.909
He makes a joke; you laugh.

00:35:21.986 --> 00:35:23.317
Is that an illusion?

00:35:23.387 --> 00:35:24.911
He says something
that makes you think.

00:35:24.989 --> 00:35:28.083
Does it matter how his
molecules are aligned?

00:35:28.159 --> 00:35:29.592
Did it ever occur to you

00:35:29.660 --> 00:35:30.752
that it's not
just a question

00:35:30.828 --> 00:35:32.625
of whether or
not he's real?

00:35:32.697 --> 00:35:34.289
What do you mean?

00:35:34.365 --> 00:35:36.094
I think you should
stop trying to control

00:35:36.167 --> 00:35:38.431
every aspect of
this relationship.

00:35:38.503 --> 00:35:40.937
Romance is born
out of differences

00:35:41.005 --> 00:35:42.939
as well as similarities...

00:35:43.007 --> 00:35:46.374
out of the unexpected
as well as the familiar.

00:35:47.678 --> 00:35:50.442
Maybe I just needed
to be sure...

00:35:50.515 --> 00:35:53.109
he'd love me back.

00:35:53.184 --> 00:35:55.448
But isn't that the
risk you always take,

00:35:55.520 --> 00:35:57.954
hologram or not?

00:35:58.022 --> 00:35:59.956
All I know is...

00:36:00.024 --> 00:36:02.720
Michael Sullivan was
up in that tree,

00:36:02.793 --> 00:36:05.227
shouting your name.

00:36:05.296 --> 00:36:08.891
I've never been afraid
of taking risks.

00:36:08.966 --> 00:36:11.230
Then perhaps next time,

00:36:11.302 --> 00:36:15.602
you should just...
let him snore.

00:36:33.357 --> 00:36:35.018
The neutronic gradient's
rising...

00:36:35.092 --> 00:36:36.320
30 million terajoules...

00:36:36.394 --> 00:36:37.486
40 million.

00:36:37.562 --> 00:36:38.494
Shields.

00:36:38.563 --> 00:36:39.825
Holding.

00:36:39.897 --> 00:36:40.864
60 million.

00:36:42.099 --> 00:36:43.157
Torres to Bridge.

00:36:43.234 --> 00:36:45.293
The inverse warp field's
destabilizing.

00:36:45.369 --> 00:36:46.666
We're losing our anchor.

00:36:46.737 --> 00:36:48.568
Acknowledged. How long
before we're clear?

00:36:48.639 --> 00:36:49.697
At least
another five minutes.

00:36:49.774 --> 00:36:50.866
That's about four

00:36:50.942 --> 00:36:51.874
minutes too long.

00:36:51.943 --> 00:36:54.036
The gradient's rising fast...
90 million.

00:36:55.479 --> 00:36:57.606
Stabilizers are off-line.

00:37:01.686 --> 00:37:03.483
Thrusters.

00:37:03.554 --> 00:37:04.646
No effect.

00:37:04.722 --> 00:37:06.121
Shields are failing.

00:37:06.190 --> 00:37:07.623
How close are we
to the perimeter?

00:37:07.692 --> 00:37:08.784
A thousand kilometers,

00:37:08.859 --> 00:37:10.827
but we're being pulled along
with the storm.

00:37:10.895 --> 00:37:11.827
What have you got in mind?

00:37:11.896 --> 00:37:13.193
The deflector beam...
we might be able

00:37:13.264 --> 00:37:14.561
to cut a path
through the wavefront.

00:37:14.632 --> 00:37:16.532
It's possible,
but we'd have to route

00:37:16.601 --> 00:37:18.398
all available power
to the emitters.

00:37:18.469 --> 00:37:20.903
That won't be enough.
Primary systems are down.

00:37:20.972 --> 00:37:23.031
Then transfer all
secondary power sources...

00:37:23.107 --> 00:37:25.905
transporters,
replicators, holodecks.

00:37:26.043 --> 00:37:27.533
Captain, there's not enough time

00:37:27.612 --> 00:37:29.546
to go through the hologrid
shutdown sequence.

00:37:29.614 --> 00:37:30.842
We'd lose most of Fair Haven.

00:37:33.317 --> 00:37:34.750
Do it.

00:37:36.153 --> 00:37:38.212
Hull fractures, Decks 6 and 7.

00:37:38.289 --> 00:37:40.189
You've got
all the secondary power, Tuvok.

00:37:40.258 --> 00:37:41.190
Is that enough?

00:37:41.259 --> 00:37:42.590
Negative.

00:37:42.660 --> 00:37:44.218
Siphon energy
from the plasma network...

00:37:44.295 --> 00:37:46.160
every last deciwatt!

00:37:46.931 --> 00:37:48.762
Deflector beam active.

00:37:53.838 --> 00:37:55.567
We're approaching the perimeter.

00:37:55.640 --> 00:37:57.232
500 kilometers...

00:37:57.308 --> 00:37:58.775
400...

00:37:59.844 --> 00:38:01.573
Deflector output is dropping.

00:38:01.646 --> 00:38:02.840
Give him everything we've got...

00:38:02.913 --> 00:38:04.505
life support,
environmental control.

00:38:04.582 --> 00:38:07.176
Scrape the residual ions off the
sonic showers if you have to.

00:38:34.278 --> 00:38:36.041
Doesn't look good, Tom.

00:38:36.113 --> 00:38:37.375
With this much photonic decay,

00:38:37.448 --> 00:38:40.383
we'll be able to save five,
maybe ten percent.

00:38:40.451 --> 00:38:42.715
So much for the
luck of the Irish.

00:38:42.787 --> 00:38:44.914
It might be easier
to start from scratch.

00:38:45.022 --> 00:38:46.250
It wouldn't be the same.

00:38:46.324 --> 00:38:47.882
It's like trying
to rewrite a novel

00:38:47.958 --> 00:38:50.825
after the only copy
of the data file's been deleted.

00:38:50.895 --> 00:38:52.487
All the details, the nuances...

00:38:52.563 --> 00:38:53.723
they're all gone.

00:38:53.798 --> 00:38:55.390
Morning, lads.

00:38:55.466 --> 00:38:56.558
Hey, Seamus.

00:38:56.634 --> 00:38:58.295
Looks like a storm brewing.

00:38:58.369 --> 00:38:59.427
A storm?

00:38:59.503 --> 00:39:00.561
Heading in from the west.

00:39:00.638 --> 00:39:02.299
Could be a big one.

00:39:02.373 --> 00:39:04.068
Oh... you're right.

00:39:04.141 --> 00:39:05.472
I don't suppose

00:39:05.543 --> 00:39:07.101
you could spare
a shilling or two?

00:39:07.178 --> 00:39:08.668
Me wife and I made up
last night,

00:39:08.746 --> 00:39:10.577
and I wanted
to buy her a new parasol.

00:39:12.416 --> 00:39:14.008
The grid's destabilizing.

00:39:14.085 --> 00:39:15.382
We'd better get started

00:39:15.453 --> 00:39:16.920
on those repairs.

00:39:18.356 --> 00:39:20.483
So, what do we try and save?

00:39:20.558 --> 00:39:22.788
The Ox and Lamb? The church?

00:39:22.860 --> 00:39:24.555
Maggie O'Halloran?

00:39:24.628 --> 00:39:26.562
There's someone
I should talk to

00:39:26.630 --> 00:39:28.655
before we decide.

00:39:30.000 --> 00:39:30.967
Come in.

00:39:34.872 --> 00:39:37.397
Fair Haven didn't fare
too well.

00:39:37.475 --> 00:39:39.841
I'll break the news to the crew.

00:39:39.910 --> 00:39:41.343
With your permission,

00:39:41.412 --> 00:39:43.710
I'd like to try
to reconstruct the program.

00:39:43.781 --> 00:39:45.408
How long will it take?

00:39:45.483 --> 00:39:47.178
Six or seven weeks.

00:39:47.251 --> 00:39:48.548
Harry tells me

00:39:48.619 --> 00:39:49.745
that we should be able
to save

00:39:49.820 --> 00:39:52.755
about ten percent
of the existing elements.

00:39:52.823 --> 00:39:55.587
I thought you might have
a suggestion or two.

00:40:14.745 --> 00:40:16.940
Computer,
is Fair Haven character

00:40:17.014 --> 00:40:19.608
Michael Sullivan still intact?

00:40:19.683 --> 00:40:21.651
Affirmative.

00:40:24.855 --> 00:40:26.823
Activate him.

00:40:28.192 --> 00:40:29.955
Hello.

00:40:30.027 --> 00:40:32.791
You disappeared on me, Katie.

00:40:32.863 --> 00:40:35.457
I woke up, and you had gone.

00:40:35.533 --> 00:40:37.626
I had some thinking to do.

00:40:37.701 --> 00:40:39.293
Are you done?

00:40:39.370 --> 00:40:42.999
Yes.

00:40:45.676 --> 00:40:47.610
I'm leaving Fair Haven.

00:40:47.678 --> 00:40:49.475
Why?

00:40:49.547 --> 00:40:51.742
Because.

00:40:51.816 --> 00:40:54.478
That's not a very good reason.

00:40:54.552 --> 00:40:57.817
The situation is complicated.

00:40:57.888 --> 00:40:59.947
Another man?

00:41:00.024 --> 00:41:01.423
No.

00:41:01.492 --> 00:41:04.484
Your friends...

00:41:04.562 --> 00:41:06.757
they don't approve of me?

00:41:06.831 --> 00:41:08.765
They think you're charming.

00:41:11.702 --> 00:41:14.637
Are you not ready
to settle down yet?

00:41:14.705 --> 00:41:18.436
I'm as ready as I'll ever be.

00:41:20.911 --> 00:41:23.038
You're not making any sense.

00:41:23.113 --> 00:41:26.674
No, I guess I'm not.

00:41:26.750 --> 00:41:29.344
Well, I have a feeling

00:41:29.420 --> 00:41:33.481
that, uh, you won't
be forgetting us that easily.

00:41:33.557 --> 00:41:36.890
Fair Haven has that effect
on people.

00:41:39.430 --> 00:41:43.298
But there's one thing
I want you to know.

00:41:43.367 --> 00:41:46.359
I love you, Katie.

00:41:49.874 --> 00:41:52.468
I might actually
be passing this way

00:41:52.543 --> 00:41:56.639
in, uh... six or seven weeks.

00:41:56.714 --> 00:41:59.649
Maybe I'll stop by the pub.

00:42:01.886 --> 00:42:03.945
See that you do.

00:42:16.734 --> 00:42:19.202
Oh, computer, end program.

00:42:28.245 --> 00:42:29.974
Wait.

00:42:32.850 --> 00:42:37.446
I want to make one more
modification to the character.

00:42:37.521 --> 00:42:39.386
Specify.

00:42:42.426 --> 00:42:47.193
Deny Kathryn Janeway
any future access

00:42:47.264 --> 00:42:49.824
to his behavioral subroutines.

00:42:51.035 --> 00:42:53.162
Modification complete.

00:42:55.539 --> 00:42:58.007
Save program.
